ulate\" derives from the Latin *calculus*, meaning a small stone used for counting. In the ancient Near East, in Mesopotamia, in the river valleys of the Tigris and Euphrates, scribes and merchants kept tallies of grain, livestock, and trade goods by arranging small clay tokens or pebbles in patterns. These tokens, known as *calculi*, were the ancestors of every counting device that would follow. They were, in their way, the first \"data storage\" medium—physical objects representing abstract quantities, arranged in spatial configurations that encoded information.\n\nBy approximately 3000 BCE, the Sumerians and later the Babylonians had developed sophisticated systems of arithmetic, including a base",
